WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
["Pomp and Circumstance"]

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:57.000
One of the most crucial steps in the story of life on earth happened in a freshwater

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:05.000
swamp about 350 million years ago. The fish began to haul themselves out onto the land.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.240
The land at the time was covered with the first plants, very different from these

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:15.600
mangrove plants of today but nonetheless plants. And in order to get out among them,

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:21.540
the fish had to solve two problems. First of all, they had the mechanical problem of

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:26.240
hauling themselves onto land and second they had to be able to breathe once they got there.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:31.680
The way they solved the problem of hauling themselves up onto the land,

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:37.640
we can see from a small little fish which lives in these mangrove swamps today. It's

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:43.000
in no way closely related to those early fish but it does give us an idea of what

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:55.240
that scene must have been like. The mudskipper. They come up out of the water to browse on small

00:01:55.240 --> 00:02:05.440
creatures that swarm on the surface of the mud. Their front fins have jointed bones inside them

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:20.000
so that the fish can use them as legs to lever itself along. The mudskipper is not by any means

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.880
the only fish to have developed muscular fins like these. Fossils of one of the first have been

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:31.720
found in rocks laid down just before the time that backboneed animals ventured onto land. The

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:41.480
coelacanth. Did this extremely ancient fish also use its fins as legs? Unfortunately, no fossils of

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:47.760
them younger than 70 million years have ever been found and up to 40 years ago scientists concluded

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.240
that they never would be able to answer that question for certain as the fish was obviously

00:02:52.240 --> 00:03:01.000
extinct. And then in 1938 a living coelacanth was caught off the coast of South Africa. It was the

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:06.760
scientific sensation of the century but before scientists could get to it to examine its entrails

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:13.360
and see how they confirmed or denied the deductions they'd made from the very ancient fossil coelacanths,

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:21.200
the fish was already rotting and its guts were thrown away unexamined. So a huge search was

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:26.360
mounted to try and find another. Leaflets and posters were printed carrying pictures of the

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:31.400
fish and offering a great reward and distributed among the countless fishing villages up the coast

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:39.960
of Africa. But nothing. Until 14 years later a second coelacanth was caught and this time it

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:45.920
came from a place over a thousand miles away from where the first one had been landed. Here in the

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:52.440
tiny Camoro Islands, a small group lying midway between Madagascar and the coast of East Africa.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:04:00.360
The first one it seems was a stray. These waters out here are the true and only home of this

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:06.000
extraordinary and rare fish and the people who live in that tiny village down there are the

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:13.080
world's greatest experts in catching coelacanths. A villager still had a dried and shriveled coelacanth

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:20.680
which he let me see. From what we know of the habits of the living coelacanth which is not very

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:27.040
much it seems that these rare fins are the ones which are used for swimming but the front ones

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:33.840
are used for maneuvering and for helping the fish to clamber about along the rocky bottom on which

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:41.040
it lives and all the fins have fleshy bases to them. The fishermen catch them at night from

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:47.760
depths of 300 meters or so. Once hooked the fish fight valiantly and it may take all night to haul

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:54.200
one to the surface so it's usually dead on arrival and scientists have still not been able to observe

00:04:54.200 --> 00:05:01.040
one alive and then while we were in the Camorros one was caught and although it was weak it was

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:11.960
still alive when the cameras arrived. 350 million years ago fish with fins like these were cruising

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:17.400
the seas of the world. Some living in shallow waters produced descendants which eventually

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:22.360
clambered onto the land while this creatures ancestors moved down to the unchanging depths

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:34.440
there to remain unchanged themselves. The Camorrians catch one or two coelacanths every year. They use

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:40.040
not to value them very much for their flesh isn't particularly good to eat. Now however big rewards

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:45.200
are offered by scientific institutions abroad so the old man who caught this one will soon be rich

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:50.960
and some researcher in a few weeks time will be absorbed in examining the structure of this fin

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:57.280
which scientists agree must resemble very closely those limbs that first took backboneed animals

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:07.720
onto the land. But how about that second problem the problem of breathing up on land. The gills which

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:12.560
had served them very efficiently while they were swimming in water extracting dissolved oxygen

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:20.400
wouldn't work in the air. How did the fish solve that problem? Well this is East Africa and it's

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:26.760
the height of the dry season. There is not a drop of water to be found in this parched landscape

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:51.520
and yet here close by me there are fish that are living and breathing in air if only I can find them.

00:06:51.520 --> 00:07:02.520
Six months ago this was a pond several feet deep in water but as the dry season progressed

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:14.000
the water evaporated and the fish in it burrowed down into this which was soft liquid mud and is

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:39.080
now brick hard and there somewhere they cocoon and that that I looks like the nose of one poking

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:46.680
out from the mud there. Now if I take this and drop it in a tank of water it should seem as though

00:07:46.680 --> 00:08:15.600
the rains have come a little early and the fish should come to life. As the water soaks in the mud

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:31.280
softens and falls away exposing a papery cocoon of dried mucus. And there is the throat of this

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:46.800
extraordinary creature that can breathe in both air and water it's a lungfish.

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:17.160
While its water breathing apparatus the gills are getting into working order again it snatches

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:35.760
another gulp of air. It's able to breathe air because it has opening from its gut a long pouch

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:43.880
lined with blood vessels and they can absorb gaseous oxygen through its moist lining. The

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:51.080
coelacanth has no lung but it has got a simple leg that fin with a fleshy base to it supported

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:58.960
by bones. Neither it nor the lungfish therefore can be close to the ancestral creature that first

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:06.960
made the move to land. But if those two crucial elements were to occur in one animal then such a

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:14.960
creature would certainly be a strong candidate and indeed they do. This fossil fish from rocks 450

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:21.120
million years old has them both. It's called Eustanoptron and when the rock and scales around

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:27.000
the base of its fin are carefully removed you can see at the bones one close to the body then two

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:35.720
then a group of small ones exactly the pattern found in the limb of all land vertebrates. And

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:41.560
that adventurous ancestor may have been very like this but why should it have climbed out onto the

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:47.840
land? Perhaps it was forced out by droughts maybe it was tempted by food the creatures that swarmed

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:53.400
on the surface of the mud. Whatever the reason its descendants came to spend more and more of their

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:58.960
time out of water and over millions of years they evolved bodies that were more suited to life on

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:24.600
land and became the first amphibians. The vegetation of the time was very different from that of today

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:30.720
there were no flowering plants or conifers and one of the communist plants was a kind of horsetail

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:37.360
rather like these growing in the north of England except that the horsetails of that time 300 million

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:45.320
years ago grew to about 50 or 60 feet tall and formed dense forests growing in swamps. When they

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:51.440
died the horsetail trunks fell into the water and formed a kind of peat and over the years there

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:57.680
were variations in the sea level which flooded these swamps and buried the peat beneath deposits

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:05.680
of sand. Under the accumulating weight of these sediments the peat then turned into coal.

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:27.200
And in the mine you can see the sand that's been turned to stone and beneath it the compressed

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:35.320
remains of the plants and in this particular scene have been found as well the bones of some

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:42.400
of the animals that crawled in those ancient swamps. This is one of the most dramatic of them

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:50.960
it's a skull here are its huge teeth which are simple teeth rather like the peg like teeth of

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:57.440
the fish of that period. We know that this creature had a paddle-shaped tail and also

00:12:57.440 --> 00:13:06.000
four very good limbs so it really was a true amphibian and it must have been a very formidable

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:35.920
creature too because it grew to a length of about 12 feet. There are many kinds

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:48.760
of them and they dominated the land for a hundred million years. The largest amphibian

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:55.600
alive today the giant salamander from Japan grows to over a meter and a half four feet or so but

00:13:55.600 --> 00:14:06.320
even that is only a quarter as big as its ancestors. Most of its living relations the rest of the

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:11.440
salamanders and newts are very much smaller a few centimeters only from nose to tail.

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:20.680
Though newts spend much of their time out of water they don't go far from it. In early spring

00:14:20.680 --> 00:14:30.040
after hibernating they must move back into it. Their skin is permeable it doesn't retain liquid

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:36.880
very well and if they dry out they die. They also need to keep their skin moist for like most

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:42.360
amphibians they breathe through it supplementing oxygen from their lungs with more absorbed

00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:49.800
directly from the air and one final shackle keeps them tied to water they have to return to it to

00:14:49.800 --> 00:15:01.720
breed. Once in water it sheds the thin outer skin it secreted to give it some degree of protection

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:09.560
on land and takes up an existence that is much more like that of a fish. It often seems that the

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:14.980
newt is more at home here than on land and indeed it retains many characteristics of its fish

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:25.880
ancestors. The males become brightly colored and develop flamboyant crests along their backs.

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:33.120
Their courtship is in many ways reminiscent of that of fish they flex the frills along their

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:39.000
backs just as so many fish flex their fins and they beat the water with their tails sending

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:44.600
powerful currents towards the female which she detects with a line of sensors which resemble

00:15:44.600 --> 00:16:00.760
the lateral line system of the fish. Two males supporting one female she's in the middle without

00:16:00.760 --> 00:16:15.160
a crest. The female lays several hundred eggs each individually stuck to a leaf development

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:28.720
is swift the tiny white sphere elongates pigment appears and soon the young emerge and they're

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:33.760
even more fish like than their parents they have no legs and they breathe not with lungs

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:40.440
but with feathery gills but slowly their legs and lungs do develop and the newt tadpole for a

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:51.120
short period has the benefit of both ways of breathing. But there's one tadpole that remains

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:58.920
in this condition all its life. Its external gills are large and feathery and permanent.

00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:21.760
It lives in one lake in Mexico and the Aztecs called it the water monster axolotl.

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:53.920
But the most surprising thing about this overgrown eternal tadpole is that it breeds in this condition.

00:17:58.920 --> 00:18:15.320
The eggs start developing immediately the black part is the beginning of the tadpole's body which

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:20.400
will grow round and enclose the cream-colored yolk food supply for further development.

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:33.520
Though the axolotl never changes into a land-living salamander in the wild it has a close relative in

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:39.200
Mexico which retains its options. Sometimes it breeds like the axolotl but if its lake

00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:45.360
dries then it's able to turn into a normal land-living salamander. The tadpoles still

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:54.920
with their feathery gills wriggle in the tepid shallowing pools. But as time passes the gills

00:18:54.920 --> 00:19:03.840
disappear for now the animal has developed lungs and eventually the little creature hauls itself

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:11.960
up onto the mud. But many salamanders don't seem to be very enthusiastic walkers and show signs of

00:19:11.960 --> 00:19:18.980
abandoning the habit. This one from California has only tiny legs and spends most of its time

00:19:18.980 --> 00:19:25.880
burrowing under logs and stones. One entire group of amphibians has opted totally for this way of

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:32.760
life and lost their legs altogether the Sicilians. You might well confuse these creatures with large

00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:39.600
earthworms. This one comes from Southeast Asia. Its eyes are covered in skin and to replace them

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:47.040
it has small white feelers just below its sightless eye. Their bodies have become greatly

00:19:47.040 --> 00:20:02.160
elongated and they've lost all traces of limbs. Most of them don't come up to the surface except

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:07.400
at night but then you really see that they're not earthworms solidly champing their way through

00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:18.080
soil. Blind though they are they're hunters. This one comes from South America.

00:20:18.080 --> 00:20:40.720
Sicilians constitute the smallest amphibian group. Only about 160 species are known compared with

00:20:40.720 --> 00:20:47.360
over twice that number of salamanders and newts but they're so unobtrusive and so easily mistaken

00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:53.080
for large worms and therefore ignored that there may well be many more kinds still to be discovered

00:20:53.080 --> 00:20:59.200
burrowing their way through the soft damp soils of the tropics. But most of the amphibians living

00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:05.480
in the world today belong to a third group. A group that doesn't live below ground like the

00:21:05.480 --> 00:21:11.480
Sicilians but above it and far from having lost their legs they have developed their legs to an

00:21:11.480 --> 00:21:20.000
absolutely spectacular degree of frogs and toads and this is the king of them all. This is the

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:27.600
largest frog in the world the Goliath frog. It's a very rare animal. It lives only in one small part

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:37.560
of West Africa. In captivity it lives on small birds, rats or mice and even fish but in the

00:21:37.560 --> 00:21:43.640
wild it seems that its diet is not quite so ambitious. It takes dragonflies and other large

00:21:43.640 --> 00:21:51.120
insects and it also catches crabs from the bottom of the river because it's a very good swimmer with

00:21:51.120 --> 00:21:59.940
very large webs at the bottom of its feet but these huge legs these huge legs also enable it

00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:07.120
to jump very well. This particular one can jump nine or ten feet say about ten times its body

00:22:07.120 --> 00:22:13.200
length but in the kingdom of the frogs that's really nothing much. Some of the smaller frogs

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:30.960
can do very much better than that. They are dazzling athletes. When each foot is webbed to

00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:55.520
form a parachute then your leaps are spectacular indeed. This is the famous flying frog though it

00:22:55.520 --> 00:23:02.000
be more accurate to call it a glider than a flyer. Even so in one leap and glide it can cover 15

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:08.640
meters or so say a hundred times its body length. Several species have developed this talent. There's

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:23.840
one in Japan another in Malaya and this one lives in Costa Rica. Its feet are not only webbed but each

00:23:23.840 --> 00:23:30.600
toe ends in a sucker so that it can also cling to vertical leaves if it has a mind to but its

00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:54.440
unique splendor is only revealed when it leaps and opens its four extraordinary parachutes.

00:24:00.600 --> 00:24:25.520
The flying frog seeks safety by launching itself into the air. This frog takes refuge

00:24:25.520 --> 00:24:38.680
underground and its pointed nose gives it a very good start and then its legs provide a pile driving

00:24:38.680 --> 00:24:50.960
thrust. The Holy Cross toad of Australia also buries itself but it goes rear end first and

00:24:50.960 --> 00:25:06.520
with a quite different kind of leg action. It's easy to understand why they hide. Frogs to a

00:25:06.520 --> 00:25:11.720
hungry hunter must appear to be both appetizing and vulnerable with their soft defenseless bodies

00:25:11.720 --> 00:25:18.400
and indeed many of them are but some have developed defenses and very surprising ones too.

00:25:18.400 --> 00:25:24.280
This grass snake is about to tackle an ordinary European toad.

00:25:42.920 --> 00:25:47.920
The combination of standing on tiptoe and inflating its body makes it look very much

00:25:47.920 --> 00:25:58.300
bigger than it really is. Whether this frightens the snake or merely baffles it,

00:25:58.300 --> 00:26:10.440
who can say? Whatever its effect, it works. The fire-bellied toad, watch. This extraordinary

00:26:10.440 --> 00:26:15.240
posture deters predators by revealing the yellow and black pattern on the animal's stomach. A

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:20.600
combination of colors that's widely recognized in the animal world as a warning and it's not

00:26:20.600 --> 00:26:26.560
all bluff either. All amphibians have mucous glands in their skin which help in keeping them

00:26:26.560 --> 00:26:32.200
moist and some of these glands in the fire-bellied toad have been adapted to produce a bitter tasting

00:26:32.200 --> 00:26:38.920
poison. Skin has become very versatile in the amphibians for breathing, defense and it comes

00:26:38.920 --> 00:26:55.320
in all sizes, shapes and colors. In South America some frogs have developed defense so far that

00:26:55.320 --> 00:27:00.760
they've become real killers. The poison they produce in their skins is so powerful that it

00:27:00.760 --> 00:27:07.160
can paralyze a monkey or a bird immediately. There are at least 20 different kinds of poison frogs

00:27:07.160 --> 00:27:12.720
in Central and South America and conspicuousness is an important part of their defense strategy.

00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:18.640
They don't want to be eaten by mistake for it's of no value to them as individuals if their attacker

00:27:18.640 --> 00:27:24.880
dies soon after they've provided it with a meal. So they're all dressed in spectacular colors. Color

00:27:24.880 --> 00:27:30.520
is of no use at night when it can't be seen so unusually for frogs these little creatures are

00:27:30.520 --> 00:27:36.800
active in the daytime, moving boldly around the forest confident and secure in their brilliant

00:27:36.800 --> 00:27:51.320
livery. This particular species has good reason to be confident. It has the most poisonous skin

00:27:51.320 --> 00:27:58.640
secretion of all. It's only recently been discovered by science and just a tiny smear from its skin

00:27:58.640 --> 00:28:05.440
could kill a man. The local Indians in Colombia use its poison on their blowgun darts by rubbing

00:28:05.440 --> 00:28:15.720
the tips on the backs of the living frogs. One frog in Argentina has developed a unique way of

00:28:15.720 --> 00:28:20.840
safeguarding against that and at the same time keeping itself reasonably watertight when the

00:28:20.840 --> 00:28:34.360
weather gets too dry. It gives itself a varnish. There are many wax glands in its skin and when

00:28:34.360 --> 00:28:39.760
it begins to feel that its body is drying out it gives itself a good going over so as to produce

00:28:39.760 --> 00:29:05.240
a thin waterproof covering.

00:29:05.240 --> 00:29:24.680
But there is of course another opposite strategy. If you're without defenses of any kind then it

00:29:24.680 --> 00:29:28.960
may be much more effective to spend the day concealed, camouflaged as part of the leaf.

00:29:28.960 --> 00:29:39.520
Some conceal themselves not to escape but to lurk in ambush. This big toad will pounce on mice

00:29:39.520 --> 00:29:49.440
and fledglings as well as worms. Cleaning bits of earth and twigs from the worm is important for

00:29:49.440 --> 00:29:54.920
the toad has no teeth and so simply swallows whole whatever gets into its mouth and it doesn't want

00:29:54.920 --> 00:30:04.560
any hard or spiky inedible bits. A tongue that can be stuck out is an amphibian invention. No

00:30:04.560 --> 00:30:15.440
fish ever had one and very effective it is too. The blink is an essential part of swallowing.

00:30:15.440 --> 00:30:21.440
Frogs and toads have no bony base to their eye sockets so their eyeballs bulge down into

00:30:21.440 --> 00:30:26.880
their mouths. When they blink the underside of the eyes helps to squeeze the food in its mouth

00:30:26.880 --> 00:30:32.360
back towards its throat. Their tongue is not attached to the back of the mouth like ours but

00:30:32.360 --> 00:30:37.400
to the front so they can stick it out very much farther than we can which is very useful for a

00:30:37.400 --> 00:30:43.640
somewhat ungainly hunter without a neck like a toad. Its end is sticky and muscular and it

00:30:43.640 --> 00:30:50.360
grabs the worm with the underside and then the tongue has one final function. It lubricates the

00:30:50.360 --> 00:30:54.720
food so that it can be swallowed easily without scratching the delicate membranes of the throat.

00:30:54.720 --> 00:31:03.080
The eyes of the amphibians are fundamentally the same in structure as those of their fish

00:31:03.080 --> 00:31:08.320
ancestors. There was no need to change them for they work as well in air as they did in water but

00:31:08.320 --> 00:31:12.720
they have to be kept moist and clean so the amphibians have developed an ability to blink

00:31:12.720 --> 00:31:33.840
and a membrane to wipe the surface. Protection from strong light by closing the iris or by

00:31:33.840 --> 00:31:41.520
using a membrane which still lets light in. In air however you do need quite a different

00:31:41.520 --> 00:31:48.760
hearing apparatus than you need in water, eardrums and with them came a voice. Some

00:31:48.760 --> 00:31:51.680
frogs call during the day like these edible frogs.

00:31:51.680 --> 00:32:16.000
But most sing at night. Before the amphibians had crawled out of the water 300 million years ago,

00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:23.080
the only animal sounds on earth had been whirs and chirps of insects. So the first animal chorus

00:32:23.080 --> 00:32:30.800
to break the silence of the land may well have been something like this. The frogs lungs which

00:32:30.800 --> 00:32:36.920
blow air through its tiny vocal cords are relatively feeble but resonating sacks bulging

00:32:36.920 --> 00:32:42.360
from the ankle of the jaws or the throat amplify it many times so that some calls can be heard for

00:32:42.360 --> 00:32:49.360
over a mile away.

00:34:12.360 --> 00:34:30.400
The cue for these choruses is usually a change in the weather for these songs are the

00:34:30.400 --> 00:34:45.640
pelud to mating. In the tropics the trigger is usually the onset of the rainy season.

00:35:00.400 --> 00:35:12.880
As the forest is drenched so the moisture loving amphibians can get out and about seeking mates

00:35:12.880 --> 00:35:32.640
and laying eggs. In this very rare species from Costa Rica the male is yellow the female red and

00:35:32.640 --> 00:35:43.560
brown. They abandon their eggs after they've laid them to return to land. Streams and ponds

00:35:43.560 --> 00:35:48.640
and other such obvious spawning sites often swarmed with fish that will eat any eggs or

00:35:48.640 --> 00:35:53.600
young that they can find. So hundreds must be laid if just one or two are to survive.

00:35:53.600 --> 00:36:00.520
In temperate areas breeding begins when the weather warms in spring. European toads migrate

00:36:00.520 --> 00:36:05.800
from miles around to a single favorite pond and assemble there in great numbers all within a few

00:36:05.800 --> 00:36:11.920
days. The breeding period may only last a week or so and towards the end of it females with

00:36:11.920 --> 00:36:18.280
eggs still to lay become very rare and the males in their frenzied a couple with them clasp anything

00:36:18.280 --> 00:36:33.840
in the neighborhood that moves male or female and so form tangled writhing groups.

00:36:48.280 --> 00:37:17.640
These toads rely for breeding success on numbers.

00:37:17.640 --> 00:37:24.800
A single female may deposit 6,000 eggs. Mass production be it in temperate or tropical

00:37:24.800 --> 00:37:37.640
places seems to be a very effective strategy. These tadpoles for example develop from eggs

00:37:37.640 --> 00:37:44.640
that were laid in enormous numbers in pools beside this South American River. But amphibians

00:37:44.640 --> 00:37:51.360
have another option. They after all can climb up onto land and so they can lay their eggs in

00:37:51.360 --> 00:37:58.360
places that no fish could possibly reach. When they first evolved and had the land to themselves

00:37:58.360 --> 00:38:04.200
that must have been a particularly effective strategy and even today there are many frogs

00:38:04.200 --> 00:38:11.520
that go to quite extraordinary lengths in order to lay their eggs away from ponds and rivers. Of

00:38:11.520 --> 00:38:17.120
course they have to keep their eggs moist otherwise they would dry out but around this

00:38:17.120 --> 00:38:31.360
river at any rate that's not too difficult. These are the Kaitua Falls on the Potaro River in

00:38:31.360 --> 00:38:37.760
Guyana. The forest around here must be sheer paradise for frogs for here in effect there is

00:38:37.760 --> 00:38:52.080
permanent rain and quite warm rain at that. The Potaro above the falls is a hundred or so meters

00:38:52.080 --> 00:38:58.800
across and its waters fall sheer for over 200 meters. Much of these great masses of falling

00:38:58.800 --> 00:39:14.920
water turns spray and drenching mists. And as the mist comes swirling up it condenses into drops

00:39:14.920 --> 00:39:23.040
which fill the center of such parts as this and turn them into miniature ponds ideal for the frogs

00:39:23.040 --> 00:39:29.040
purposes. Inside this particular one there's a tiny beautiful golden frog.

00:39:35.040 --> 00:39:41.320
It shares its minute pool with a few insect larvae but with nothing that does it or it takes any harm.

00:39:41.320 --> 00:39:55.720
There are many such plants with water-filled chalices in their centers in the South American

00:39:55.720 --> 00:40:01.360
rainforest and many of them grow not on the ground but high up perched on branches with

00:40:01.360 --> 00:40:07.700
their roots dangling in the moist air. So they are in effect ponds up trees and they provide the

00:40:07.700 --> 00:40:13.960
frogs with little oases where they can live and spawn away from predators generation after

00:40:13.960 --> 00:40:24.080
generation without ever coming down to the ground. In the African savannas where there's much less

00:40:24.080 --> 00:40:29.760
rain there are no such plants but there is nonetheless a frog that manages to breed up in

00:40:29.760 --> 00:40:45.200
the trees instead of water it uses foam. The trick is done at night. The female excretes a special

00:40:45.200 --> 00:40:50.940
liquid which she beats into a lather with her hind legs. The male joins her and fertilizes the

00:40:50.940 --> 00:40:57.680
150 or so eggs which she deposits in the foam. The Sun will bake the outside into a hard crust

00:40:57.680 --> 00:41:05.360
but inside it remains liquid and there the eggs develop. The nests are always made above water so

00:41:05.360 --> 00:41:11.080
in due time when the crust cracks and the young ooze out they drop straight down into a river or a

00:41:11.080 --> 00:41:41.040
swamp. This frog from South America

00:41:41.040 --> 00:41:46.880
also has a way of keeping its eggs away from the dangers of the river. Here however where the air

00:41:46.880 --> 00:41:53.600
is more humid it doesn't need foam because the jelly surrounding its eggs doesn't dry out. The

00:41:53.600 --> 00:41:58.680
young tadpoles develop inside the jelly as the young of many other species do but these stay

00:41:58.680 --> 00:42:14.760
there while they go through most of their larval development. They even develop gills inside the

00:42:14.760 --> 00:42:32.320
egg and their hearts begin to beat. Eventually they too will emerge and drop down into water.

00:42:32.320 --> 00:42:39.560
The tadpoles of one Caribbean frog have managed astonishingly to dispense with water altogether

00:42:39.560 --> 00:42:45.240
for their development. The whistling frog lays a cluster of eggs on the ground. They're only

00:42:45.240 --> 00:42:51.440
small but inside each there is liquid and in it the young develop not only to the tadpole stage

00:42:51.440 --> 00:43:14.120
but beyond. Their tiny stomachs are full of yolk that must fuel their entire development. The

00:43:14.120 --> 00:43:30.960
front legs are formed and so are the back legs and at last it becomes virtually a tiny fully

00:43:30.960 --> 00:43:51.240
developed replica of its parent. On its nose it has a tiny spike and with that it punctures

00:43:51.240 --> 00:43:57.520
the egg membrane and after about 18 days it hatches having eliminated altogether the tadpole's

00:43:57.520 --> 00:44:04.360
normal need for open water. Laying your eggs away from water and all its dangers is one very

00:44:04.360 --> 00:44:11.440
successful breeding strategy but yet other frogs have taken a different line. Instead of abandoning

00:44:11.440 --> 00:44:17.040
their eggs in as safer places they can find they stay with them and look after them. The midwife

00:44:17.040 --> 00:44:22.600
toad lives in Europe. Its name is not very accurate because it's not the female but the male that

00:44:22.600 --> 00:44:28.720
carries the eggs entangled around his legs. There may be 60 or so of them and he carries them for

00:44:28.720 --> 00:44:34.440
six or seven weeks. When the moment comes for hatching he takes them down to water and the

00:44:34.440 --> 00:44:44.080
tadpole simply swim away. Some toads spend all their time in water. This peeper from Brazil

00:44:44.080 --> 00:44:54.480
instead of laying and abandoning 6,000 eggs like the European toad lays a mere hundred or so. But

00:44:54.480 --> 00:45:00.400
they look after them in the most extraordinary manner. The male with these elegant and careful

00:45:00.400 --> 00:45:05.480
movements of its hind feet takes care that as many eggs as possible are gathered together on

00:45:05.480 --> 00:45:14.680
the females back and they stick.

00:45:35.480 --> 00:45:48.120
Then the skin on the females back begins to swell. The eggs rapidly become embedded in it.

00:45:53.120 --> 00:46:03.080
Soon a membrane grows over them to enclose them completely. After only 30 hours almost all the

00:46:03.080 --> 00:46:09.760
eggs have disappeared entirely and the skin is complete again. After nearly three weeks it's

00:46:09.760 --> 00:46:33.600
moving and then after another three weeks or so the young begin to emerge.

00:46:39.760 --> 00:46:51.280
Now the parent leaves the young to fend for themselves but at least they're now independent

00:46:51.280 --> 00:46:57.360
swimmers able to find hiding places for themselves. So a much higher proportion is likely to survive

00:46:57.360 --> 00:47:04.040
than would have happened if they had been abandoned as eggs. This little South American

00:47:04.040 --> 00:47:09.680
frog also keeps her eggs and young on her back in a pouch with an opening just above the base of

00:47:09.680 --> 00:47:23.000
her spine. Her developing young remain inside it for three months or more until at last she

00:47:23.000 --> 00:47:35.960
releases them into a pool as tadpoles. They're already on their way towards their final change

00:47:35.960 --> 00:47:47.080
into the adult form for they have their back legs. These many differing ways of carrying their

00:47:47.080 --> 00:47:52.520
developing young may seem extraordinary enough but some other frogs in South America actually

00:47:52.520 --> 00:47:59.880
retain their tadpoles inside their bodies and in the most unlikely parts. There is one which

00:47:59.880 --> 00:48:24.240
calls in the beach forests of Southern Chile. It's Darwin's frog. These little creatures only a few

00:48:24.240 --> 00:48:30.720
centimeters long are all males even though they vary in color. They're still calling but the actual

00:48:30.720 --> 00:48:37.600
breeding is over. The females have laid their eggs in groups of 20 or 30 on the forest floor. As soon

00:48:37.600 --> 00:48:43.920
as the males see a movement in the eggs they will apparently eat them. Each male may take a dozen or

00:48:43.920 --> 00:48:49.200
so but he doesn't swallow them. Instead they go into his vocal sac that runs down the front of

00:48:49.200 --> 00:48:55.840
his throat and there they develop and wriggle. The males sit about struck dumb by their own

00:48:55.840 --> 00:49:19.840
offspring but eventually after some weeks their extraordinary vocal pregnancy comes to an end.

00:49:19.840 --> 00:49:37.800
And here is that amazing birth once again in slow motion. The males vocal sac is now ready

00:49:37.800 --> 00:49:47.320
again for singing before it's turned next season once more into a nursery. But the prize for the

00:49:47.320 --> 00:49:52.720
care of the young must surely go to this frog that lives only on a remote mountain in West Africa.

00:49:52.720 --> 00:50:02.740
The female only about a centimeter long keeps the eggs inside her distended oviducts and holds them

00:50:02.740 --> 00:50:08.520
there throughout the nine months of the dry season. As they grow she secretes internally some

00:50:08.520 --> 00:50:15.760
white flakes. The tadpoles moving around freely inside the oviduct eat the flakes and digest them

00:50:15.760 --> 00:50:21.880
in their gut just as they would do if they were outside in the pond. When the rain comes she gives

00:50:21.880 --> 00:50:28.720
birth. Her stomach and oviduct don't have muscles which can contract and expel the young so she does

00:50:28.720 --> 00:50:34.520
it by bracing her body against the ground with her forelegs and inflating her lungs so that they

00:50:34.520 --> 00:50:41.440
bulge down into her abdomen and squeeze the young out. And they're born fully formed froglets a real

00:50:41.440 --> 00:50:48.640
triumph of parental care. By providing their young with moisture of some kind and using all

00:50:48.640 --> 00:50:54.560
these varying and astonishing techniques frogs and toads have managed to colonize almost all the

00:50:54.560 --> 00:51:00.480
world. Even so you would think that with their thin permeable moist skins they would never be

00:51:00.480 --> 00:51:06.160
able to survive in the parched desert of Australia. But one or two species managed to live even here.

00:51:06.160 --> 00:51:11.860
They spend nearly all their lives buried deep in the ground away from the murdering sun. They may

00:51:11.860 --> 00:51:31.120
lie here for years waiting but eventually the rains do come. When the frogs burrowed down here

00:51:31.120 --> 00:51:36.240
during the last rains they were bloated with water and they've conserved it in their underground

00:51:36.240 --> 00:51:42.160
chambers by sealing themselves inside a membrane secreted from their skins. But when the rain

00:51:42.160 --> 00:52:05.080
arrives again they must quickly get rid of their watertight packaging to be ready for breeding.

00:52:05.080 --> 00:52:10.800
In the brief period when the desert is wet these frogs will dig themselves out and mate

00:52:10.800 --> 00:52:15.920
and their tadpoles will develop in the few days that there's water in the temporary pools. And

00:52:15.920 --> 00:52:22.880
then as the desert dries out the young frogs will bury themselves and remain underground for perhaps

00:52:22.880 --> 00:52:38.480
five years or more. In times of drought the desert aborigines search eagerly for frogs like this and

00:52:38.480 --> 00:52:43.840
this is why. If you squeeze one you can get quite a reasonable drink of water from it.

00:53:00.720 --> 00:53:07.880
It's tasteless and really quite drinkable. And now I'm going to have to find a pond for this little

00:53:07.880 --> 00:53:13.880
creature where it will survive until the next rains come. For the fact of the matter is that

00:53:13.880 --> 00:53:20.040
its success as a desert liver is very limited. It can only be active and breed during that very

00:53:20.040 --> 00:53:26.480
short period when there's rain. In order to survive in a desert and breed there if necessary

00:53:26.480 --> 00:53:37.600
without any rain at all you need a device that no frog or amphibian has got. This. An egg with

00:53:37.600 --> 00:53:44.360
a waterproof shell. That was the next great evolutionary breakthrough and it was achieved

00:53:44.360 --> 00:53:53.720
by the reptiles. The astonishment is that without it the amphibians managed to colonize so much of

00:53:53.720 --> 00:54:09.560
the world.
